This guide ensures consistency, clarity, and quality across all documentation contributions. When reviewing pre-existing content, remember to retain key details through any edits.

## 📝 Writing Standards

### Voice and Tone

**Required Style:** Present tense, second person, imperative mood

✅ **Correct:** "Click the Share button to export your dashboard."  
❌ **Incorrect:** "Users should click the Share button when they want to export their dashboards."

### Point of View

You are writing directly to the **customer** and **telling them what to do**. Be clear, direct, and action-oriented.

**Example:**
> To get started using Zoë, just ask her a question. If you don't know what you can ask about, simply ask what data she can see, or what would be a great question to ask next. When you're asking for data, be specific (e.g. "Show me sales YTD compared to the prior YTD, broken out by product type") or general (e.g. "I don't really know what I want to see, but tell me about channel and campaign performance"). Zoë can handle both types of questions, and will ask you follow-up questions if she isn't able to make reasonable assumptions about what you intend from your question.

### General Writing Guidelines

- **Use active voice:** "Configure the dashboard" not "The dashboard should be configured"
- **Start with verbs:** "Create a new workflow" not "To create a new workflow"
- **Avoid jargon:** Use simple, clear language accessible to all skill levels
- **Define acronyms:** Spell out on first use, then use the acronym

## 📁 File Structure and Organization

### Markdown Files (.md)

1. **Add to existing structure** unless you have a compelling reason to create new sections
2. **Use markdown preview** in your IDE before committing
3. **Follow naming conventions:**
   - Use lowercase with hyphens: `getting-started.md`, `api-reference.md`
   - Be descriptive but concise: `workflow-automation.md` not `how-to-use-workflow-automation-features.md`

### Content Organization Ideas

Organize content by one of these logical structures:

#### By Component
- Sharing functionality
- Drawer interface
- Chat Input
- History Sidebar
- Dashboard Builder
- Query Editor

#### By User Persona
- Self-serve question-answering workflows
- Rigorous exploratory modeling during setup
- Admin configuration tasks
- Developer API integration

#### By CRUD Operations
- Creating resources
- Reading/viewing data
- Updating configurations
- Deleting items

### Section Headers

- Use sentence case for headers: "Setting up your first dashboard" not "Setting Up Your First Dashboard"
- Keep headers descriptive and searchable
- Limit to three levels of hierarchy (H1, H2, H3)
- Include keywords users might search for

## 🖼️ Images and Assets

### Where assets live

Every image belongs in its own section's asset directory: `<section>/.gitbook/assets/`.

This is the only directory GitBook writes to — images uploaded through the GitBook editor land there and sync back to git. A custom folder is git-only, so a mixed team ends up with assets in two places and only one of them maintained.

### Assets do not cross sections

A section resolves assets only within its own project directory. `docs/.gitbook/assets/` is not visible to `developer/`, and there is no shared pool.

For an image used in more than one section, either:

- **Duplicate it** into each section's asset directory — preferred, keeps the repo self-contained
- **Reference an absolute URL** to an externally hosted copy — avoids duplication, but adds an external dependency

Cross-section *page* links follow the same rule: use absolute URLs, since relative paths like `../data-modeling/foo.md` do not resolve across a section boundary.

### Video and animation

Referencing an `.mp4` with image syntax does **not** produce a video player — GitBook renders it as a clickable download link. Inline playback requires an embed block pointing at a publicly accessible URL:

```
{% embed url="https://..." %}
```

That URL cannot be a local repository path, so video must be hosted before it can be embedded.

Practically, an animated GIF is the only inline motion that can be authored entirely from git. Keep them short and small — GitBook's own guidance for video is to stay under 2MB, and a multi-megabyte GIF is a slow page for every reader. For anything longer than a few seconds, host the video and use an embed block.

The upload cap is **100MB per file**, and it applies to Git Sync as well as editor uploads.

### Naming

- Descriptive kebab-case: `user-roles-matrix.png`, not `Screenshot 2025-07-27 at 10.11.16 AM.png`
- No spaces — they require quoting and break shell globs and scripts
- Rename auto-generated screenshot names when you touch the page

### Before adding an image

- Confirm it is actually referenced by a page; unreferenced assets accumulate silently
- Prefer PNG for screenshots and static diagrams
- Crop to the relevant UI rather than capturing the full window
